            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"DIVERGENT\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"The consensus primarily identifies the 'great fish' (especially Jonah's) as a typological symbol of the grave, Sheol, and death, prefiguring Christ's burial and resurrection. A recognized secondary strand interprets 'sea-monsters' (Leviathan, Rahab) as representing cosmic chaos or hostile pagan empires.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"The great fish or sea-monster represents a great human ruler who acts as the personal head of a kingdom or state (such as Pharaoh, Nebuchadnezzar, or Caesar), who 'swallows' people through his administration and is later forced to give them back.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"The grave/tomb (typology of death and resurrection) or an instrument of divine providence.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"A great ruler among the sea of peoples (a personal head of state).\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"CONTRADICTS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"DIFFERENT_REFERENT\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The book completely displaces the primary consensus view of the fish as the tomb/Sheol, elevating what the consensus considers a secondary, distinct category (Old Testament sea-monsters as hostile empires) to be the universal core meaning, even for New Testament fulfillments.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"The consensus states the fish symbolizes the grave or an instrument of divine deliverance pointing to Christ's resurrection. The book explicitly rejects this (as noted in its 'Common-View Note') and claims the fish symbolizes a political ruler/state head.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"While the consensus acknowledges 'sea-monsters' can represent nations like Egypt or Babylon, it strictly separates this from the 'great fish' of Jonah, which it identifies as the tomb. The book conflates these, discarding the 'tomb' interpretation entirely and applying the 'ruler/empire' meaning to the fish/sea-monster symbol universally.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"Matthew 12:40 explicitly parallels Jonah's time in the fish's belly with the Son of Man being 'in the heart of the earth' (the grave), which provides direct scriptural support for the consensus's death/tomb typology rather than a human ruler.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
